{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1784646302,"nonce":"s5DZH63aCD90VdKOdu6+9o9Px7E=","message":"Root-cause analysis (no fix proposed).\n\nFalse-positive series match on the **no-Change-ID backward-discovery** path.\n\nA series without a Change-ID gets a synthetic one\n(`src/b4/review/tracking.py:563-568`: `{date}-{slug}-{fingerprint[:12]}`). To\nfind older revisions, the mailbox search falls back to a fuzzy subject+from\nquery (`src/b4/mbox.py:757-761`: `(s:\"\u003csubject\u003e\" AND f:\"\u003cemail\u003e\")`). Two\ndistinct series by the same author with similar subjects -- here both `asoc-*`\nfrom rdunlap -- both match this query.\n\nThe block that would reject cross-series results\n(`src/b4/mbox.py:841`, guarded by `if by_changeid and change_ids:`) only runs\non the change-id search path. On the subject+from fallback (`by_changeid` is\nFalse) it is skipped, so the unrelated series' messages are added to the\nmailbox unchecked.\n\n`find_existing_change_id()` (`src/b4/review/tracking.py:1108-1120`) then matches\nany discovered revision by `message_id` against the `series`/`revisions`\ntables. The unrelated v1 is already tracked, so its change_id is returned and\nthe newly tracked series is recorded as a revision of the wrong series.\n\nThis is the inverse of the too-weak-matching bugs 47d5a4c (Series revision\nidentification issue) and 2235355 (Finding older versions when adding\ntracking): here matching is too aggressive on the fuzzy path.\n`absorb_series_as_revision` / manual revision linking is the recovery route.","files":null}]}